      "content": "cool. lets say we have a group of 5 professionals at a networking mixer. subject A has high facial symmetry but a very flat, monotonic vocal frequency. subject B has slightly lower symmetry but a highly dynamic range in their pitch and resonance. according to my formula, subject B should actually command more perceived status in this specific environment despite the visual deficit. do you see the mathematical logic in why vocal variance would act as a multiplier for the symmetry coefficient in a high-stakes social setting?",
